          %h3#invariant_polynomials Invariant polynomials
          %p
            By the above discussion of homotopy groups, it follows (by
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Weil+theory"} Chern-Weil theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomials"} invariant polynomials
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔢
                    %mn 8
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{e}_8
            are the quadratic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+form"} Killing form
            and then next an octic polynomial. That is described in (
            %a{:href => "#CederwallPalmkvist"}> Cederwall-Palmkvist
            ).
          %h3#as_uduality_of_3d_sugra As U-duality of 3d SuGra
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 8
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_8
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/U-duality"} U-duality
            group (see there) of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} compactified
            to 3 dimensions.
